{"generated_at": "2026-10-19T18:49:31.761546+00:00", "slug": "issue-tort-reform-live", "title": "Lawsuits, nuclear verdicts and civil justice, 19 days of posts", "stats": {"posts_processed": 3, "claims": 6, "clusters": 5, "checkable": 4, "verified": 2, "unsupported_spreading": 0, "cross_platform": 0, "news_checked": 0, "crossed_over": 0}, "clusters": [{"event": null, "event_type": null, "news": null, "id": 1, "text": "Lawsuit abuse costs each Texas family about $4,600 a year in hidden costs.", "type": "fact", "checkable": true, "stance": "for", "entities": ["Texas"], "verdict": "misleading", "verdict_basis": "The roughly $4,600 figure traces to the U.S. Chamber's Institute for Legal Reform (ILR) tort-cost study. \nILR put Texas tort costs at nearly $38 billion in 2022, an average of $4,594 per household.\n Texans for Lawsuit Reform (TLR) and the Insurance Council of Texas repeat it. TLR's roundup rounds it to \nabout $4,600 per household\n. The claim has three problems.\n   - What the number measures. ILR's \"tort costs\" are defined as \nlitigation expenses, claimant payouts and administrative fees\n. That covers all insurable tort costs, including legitimate claims, and is not a measure of \"lawsuit abuse\". ILR also says its figure is limited to \ninsurable tort costs\n.\n   - Who produced it. The estimate comes from a business-lobby group and is promoted by a tort-reform advocacy group. The study covers 2022 and is a household figure, not a family figure, so it is not current \"a year\" data.\n   - Competing estimates. Other advocacy-linked estimates for \"lawsuit abuse\" in Texas differ. These include \n$1,725 per person\n and a Perryman Group study giving \n$5,549 per Texas family\n. A Washington Examiner op-ed puts it at \n$1,943 per Texas resident\n. So the figure is one contested estimate, not an estab", "verdict_sources": ["https://instituteforlegalreform.com/blog/the-burden-of-lawsuit-costs-on-texas-households-continues-to-rise/", "https://www.tortreform.